## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing an augmented state space and applying Dijkstra with a resource constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra on expanded state space
- Edge relaxation with/without boost
- Path reconstruction from 3D predecessor structure

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithBoosts(adj, s, t, K){
  const n = adj.length; const INF = 1e15;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const parent = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(K+1).fill(null));
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  // min-heap assumed
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({d:0,u:s,k:0});
  while(!pq.empty()){
    const {d,u,k} = pq.pop();
    if(d !== dist[u][k]) continue;
    for(const {to,w} of adj[u]){
      if(dist[to][k] > d + w){
        dist[to][k] = d+w; parent[to][k] = {u,k};
        pq.push({d: dist[to][k], u:to, k});
      }
      if(k < K){
        const ww = Math.floor(w/2);
        if(dist[to][k+1] > d + ww){
          dist[to][k+1] = d+ww; parent[to][k+1] = {u,k};
          pq.push({d: dist[to][k+1], u:to, k:k+1});
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = 0; for(let kk=1; kk<=K; kk++) if(dist[t][kk] < dist[t][best]) best = kk;
  return {dist: dist[t][best], used: best, parent};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for negative weights with a limited number of boosts? 
- How would you verify correctness on large graphs with high K?

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about DAGs, timing, and dynamic programming over graphs, a common pattern in build pipelines, data workflows, or task scheduling.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort to linearize a DAG
- DP on DAG to propagate earliest start times
- In-degree based BFS (Kahn) and cycle detection

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestTimes(n, duration, edges) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const es = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let visited = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    visited++;
    for (const v of g[u]) {
      es[v] = Math.max(es[v], es[u] + duration[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const total = Math.max(...es.map((s, i) => s + duration[i]));
  return { earliestStart: es, totalDuration: total };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to detect cycles and report the cycle path?
- How would you extend to handle parallel resources or varying durations per edge?
